8. PRODUCT STRATEGY AND CORE EXPERIENCE
8.1 The Core Mentorship Lifecycle
Interlink's entire architecture and user experience should support seven stages.
Stage 1: Discover
Users explore mentors based on professional expertise, industry, experience and availability.
Stage 2: Connect
Mentees submit structured requests and mentors review them.
Stage 3: Establish
Both participants agree on expectations, communication preferences, objectives and duration.
Stage 4: Learn
Participants communicate, schedule sessions and exchange relevant resources.
Stage 5: Apply
Mentees work towards agreed development objectives.
Stage 6: Track
Progress is documented through milestones, feedback and reviews.
Stage 7: Grow
The relationship generates measurable outcomes and can be completed, renewed or extended.
8.2 Mentorship Relationship as the Central Product Entity
The mentorship relationship must be the central organising entity within the platform.
Goals, milestones, conversations, sessions, resources and reviews should all be associated with a specific mentorship relationship.
This ensures that activity is contextualised and that users do not lose track of the purpose of individual interactions.
8.3 Dedicated Mentorship Workspace
Every accepted mentorship relationship should create a dedicated workspace.
The workspace should contain:
Mentor and mentee information.
Relationship status.
Mentorship objectives.
Goals and milestones.
Upcoming sessions.
Conversation history.
Shared resources.
Progress updates.
Relationship activity history.
Completion and feedback options.
8.4 Relationship Lifecycle
The system must support:
Pending activation.
Active.
Paused.
Completed.
Terminated.
A relationship should not automatically become completed simply because there has been no activity.
Inactive relationships should instead be identified for review.

