27. FINAL PRODUCT RECOMMENDATIONS
The following recommendations should guide product decisions throughout development and launch.
27.1 Make the Mentorship Lifecycle the Core Product
The platform must make it possible for users to:
Discover a mentor → Request mentorship → Establish a relationship → Set goals → Schedule sessions → Track progress → Complete mentorship.
Every MVP feature should support this journey.
27.2 Solve Mentor Supply Before Scaling Mentee Acquisition
A platform with many mentees and insufficient mentors will create frustration.
Interlink should recruit credible mentors before investing heavily in large-scale user acquisition.
27.3 Introduce a Strong Mentor Verification Framework
Professional credibility is central to the platform's value proposition.
Verification must be transparent, meaningful and accessible.
27.4 Prioritise Mentor Availability and Capacity
Mentors should control when they accept requests and how many active mentees they can support.
This protects mentor experience and improves response rates.
27.5 Make Mentorship Workspaces the Centre of Engagement
Each active mentorship should have one dedicated environment containing its objectives, sessions, conversations, resources and progress.
This should be one of Interlink's defining product experiences.
27.6 Introduce Structured Relationship Completion
Mentorship should have an intentional ending or renewal process.
Participants should be able to review achievements, document outcomes and decide whether to continue.
27.7 Build for Outcomes, Not Activity
Do not measure success only through messages, registrations or profile views.
Measure meaningful relationships, completed sessions, goal progress and participant satisfaction.
27.8 Keep the MVP Technically Manageable
Use:
Angular.
NestJS.
PostgreSQL.
Prisma.
Managed infrastructure.
Third-party email and storage services.
A modular monolithic architecture.
Avoid premature microservices and unnecessary infrastructure.
27.9 Make Trust and Safety Non-Negotiable
Reporting, blocking, verification, moderation and administrative audit logs must exist in the initial product.
27.10 Preserve Free Mentorship Access
The platform should build trust and demonstrate value before introducing paid services.
Commercial opportunities should complement the core mission.
27.11 Launch With a Controlled Pilot
Before a broad launch, test the product with a smaller community.
Observe actual behaviour rather than relying entirely on assumptions.
27.12 Build a Product That Learns From Its Users
Interlink should continuously evaluate:
Where users abandon onboarding.
Which mentors attract relevant requests.
Why mentors reject requests.
How long relationships remain active.
Which goals are completed.
What causes mentorship relationships to end.
What users consider valuable.
These findings should guide future development.

28. CONCLUSION
Interlink is envisioned as more than a platform for finding professional contacts.
It is a structured environment where experience meets ambition, knowledge becomes guidance and professional relationships translate into measurable growth.
Its fundamental purpose is to make mentorship more accessible, intentional, credible and accountable.
By combining mentor discovery, professional verification, structured mentorship relationships, communication, development objectives and progress tracking, Interlink can address an important gap between individuals seeking career direction and experienced professionals willing to provide it.
The recommended strategy is to:
Establish a credible mentor community.
Launch a focused mentorship-first MVP.
Validate the complete mentorship lifecycle.
Build trust through verification and platform governance.
Measure actual mentorship outcomes.
Improve the product using real user feedback.
Expand progressively into career development, institutional partnerships and intelligent recommendations.
The long-term success of Interlink will not be determined by the number of features it introduces, but by the number of people whose professional journeys are meaningfully improved through the platform.

